Class Balancing for 5 Simuli------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Underfitting:
  - Happens if C is too small (strong regularization), not enough neurons/features or model is too constrained
Overfitting:
  - Happens if C is too large (weak regularization, too many features relative to samples, model memorizes training data

New Data distribution:
    - Cinematic: 128 samples
    - Rendered: 128 samples
    - Sports1m: 128 samples
    - monet2: 40 samples
    - trippy: 40 samples

    Without balancing, models easiest strategy is always predict one of the big 3 classes and get 77% accuracy just by ignoring minority classes

    Solution 1:
    class_weight="balanced"
      - Mathematically adjusts the loss function to penalize mistakes on minority classes more heavily
        normal loss: error_cinematic + error_rendered + error_sports1m + error_monet2 + error_trippy
        weighted loss : 1 * error_cinematic + 1*error_rendered + 1*error_sports1m + 3.2*error_monet2 + 3.2*error_trippy
            3.2 = 128/40 ---> the raito of majority to minority
      - simple, uses all training samples, does not give the model more minority examples to learn from

    Solution 2:
    Undersampling, RandomUnderSamples(random_state=42)
      - Randomly removes samples from majority classes to match minority classes size
      - throws away 88 samples form each majoirty class. loss of 264 samples
      - may underfit beacuse the model doesnt see enough majorty class variation

    Solution 3:
    Oversampling, RandomOverSampler(random_state=42)
      - Randomly duplicates samples from minority classes to match majority classes size
      - Change from 40 samples to 128 by just duplicating random trials in those 40 original samples
      - Creates exact duplications no new information, can lead to overfitting on minority classes


Balanced Accuracy vs Regular Accuracy

  REgular Accuracy:
    - correct_preditions / total_preditions but this can be bad since if monet2 and trippy are never predicted correctly the model
      can ignore those and still get a higher accuracy than it really does. 
  Balanced Accuracy:
    - For each class, compute the recall (true positive rate) and take the average of the recalls. It will show the accuracy 
      included in the fact that some monet2 and trippy were never classified. 


PCA and tPCA notes------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

PCA (Principal Component Analysis)
  Imagine you are describing the location of houses you can use, street adress, distance from downtown, distance from target, latitude, longitude
  elevation, distance from subway. That is seven numbers per house but many of them are redundant. PCA finds the best compressed representaiton. 
  Maybe you only need 2-3 numbers to describe a house instead of 7. 

  - Current Data:
      Each trial looks like: [neuorn1_rate, neuron2_rate, neuorn3_rate,....] --> [0.52, 1.23, 0.01, ....]
      Nueron1 and Neuron2 might always fire together or neuonr 4 and neuron8 might be anit correlated (one up one down) PCA discovers that there arent really
      200 independent peices of information here the neural population actually varies along something much smaller. 

  - Mathematical Process:
      Center the data (subtract mean of each neuron)
      Compute covariance matrix (how neurons are correclated or anti correlated)
      Find eigenvectors (principal components) -> the "main axis" of variation captures the most variance in the data
      Each PC is a weighted combination of all neurons that caputes the fundamental pattern of population activity
      Project data onto PCs

tPCA (temporal PCA)
  Finds patterns across TIME within each trial
  Input: [trials x time x neurons] --> [trials x (time*neurons)]
  Each trial = full time seies of neural activity
  Finds how neural acvity evolve over time

Perhaps you can use both PCA and tPCA maybe tPCA on full temporal data --> 100 temporal feaures then PCA on those 100 feautres --> 30 final features


Two Separate Experiements:
  Mean Features + Regular PCA: extracts mean firing rate, tests with no PCA, PCA20, PCA50, PCA100
  Temporal Features + tPCA: extracts full temporal traces (200 nueonrs x 200 timepoints = 40,000 features) always uses PCA because temporal data is too high dimensional
      preserves temporal dynamics of nueral activity

  - If tPCA has higher accuracy the brains temporal response patterns are important to distinguish different stimuli, if mean has higher accuracy then just
    the average activity level is enough, timing doesnt matter


